_email="lhchavez@google.com" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="aosp/platform/system/connectivity/shill" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/aosp/platform/system/connectivity/shill" ref="refs/changes/08/402208/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="1"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-4.4" change_id="Ia8d79516edd953c14b556dafb5296cb112f26ee1" commit="d440ad94b61fed910ad138c17b0696ee504211b8" commit_message="UPSTREAM: tpm_crb: fix crb_req_canceled behavior  The req_canceled() callback is used by tpm_transmit() periodically to check whether the request has been canceled while it is receiving a response from the TPM.  The TPM_CRB_CTRL_CANCEL register was cleared already in the crb_cancel callback, which has two consequences:  * Cancel might not happen. * req_canceled() always returns zero.  A better place to clear the register is when starting to send a new command. The behavior of TPM_CRB_CTRL_CANCEL is described in the section 5.5.3.6 of the PTP specification.  CC: stable@vger.kernel.org Fixes: 30fc8d138e91 (&quot;tpm: TPM 2.0 CRB Interface&quot;) Signed-off-by: Jarkko Sakkinen &lt;jarkko.sakkinen@linux.intel.com&gt; (cherry picked from commit 72fd50e14e46dc0edf360631bdece87c2f066a97)  BUG=chromium:658502 TEST=kernel_TPMPing  Change-Id: Ia8d79516edd953c14b556dafb5296cb112f26ee1 Signed-off-by: Guenter Roeck &lt;groeck@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/402188 Reviewed-by: Andrey Pronin &lt;apronin@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="402188" owner_email="groeck@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/88/402188/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="Iffda57717fd2f2f5ef1f54d0e5e9189edbcffdb5" commit="36e35c427fa064bd642d21e6536954ce599b8198" commit_message="[autotest] Fix certain broken unit tests, and make sure th